# RULING OWED — CI cannot publish the unscoped `cratylus` package

**Raised 2026-08-07 by mav, mid-`/carry-on`. Everything on my side of the line is
done and green; this is the one thing I hold no credential for.**

## The fork

`cratylus@0.2.1` will not publish from CI. The release job fails with:

```
npm error code E403
npm error 403 Forbidden - PUT https://registry.npmjs.org/cratylus
          - You may not perform that action with these credentials.
```

Every **scoped** package publishes fine in the same run — `@cratylus/forge@0.3.0`
went out with sigstore provenance thirty seconds later. Only the **unscoped** name
is refused.

## Why it is a credential fact and not a code fact

Diagnosed by asking the registry rather than reading the workflow:

| package               | attestation | ⇒ published by     |
| --------------------- | ----------- | ------------------ |
| `cratylus@0.2.0`      | **NONE**    | a human, by hand   |
| `@cratylus/forge@0.3.0` | sigstore provenance | this CI job |

`npm owner ls` shows the same single owner (`lcar`) for both, so it is not an
ownership problem. **CI has never once published the unscoped package.** It looked
like it had, because a hand-publish put `0.2.0` on the registry and the release job
has reported success ever since by *skipping* a version that was already there.

Most likely cause: the `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` is a **granular token granted on the
`@cratylus` scope**, which covers every `@cratylus/*` package and does not cover a
standalone name. A 2FA-required-on-publish setting on that one package would
produce the same 403. Both are settings on npmjs.com.

## What only you can do

Either, on npmjs.com:

1. **Widen the token** — add the `cratylus` package to the granular token's package
   list (or reissue the token with both the `@cratylus` scope and that package); or
2. **Configure a trusted publisher** for `cratylus` pointing at this repo's
   `release` workflow — which the release workflow's own header already names as the
   destination once every package exists, and would let `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` be
   deleted.

**(2) is the better answer** and is what the workflow was written to end at. (1) is
the faster one.

## What is already true, so this is the only blocker

- `main` is green: 16/16 tasks, 950 tests, skip delta unchanged.
- `@cratylus/forge@0.3.0` — the omp adapter — **is on npm with provenance**.
- `cratylus@0.2.1` is versioned, tagged in the changelog, packed and audited by
  `pack:smoke` in that same run. It reached the registry and was refused.

## Why I did not route around it

Publishing by hand from this workstation would work and is the wrong act twice
over: it bypasses the sigstore provenance the release workflow exists to produce
(which is how the hand-published `0.2.0` came to be indistinguishable from a CI
publish, and is the reason this went undiagnosed), and it spends a credential that
is yours, not mine.

## What it blocks

The coal validation. `cratylus@0.2.0` is what `npm i -g cratylus` still installs,
and its dependency range does not reach `@cratylus/forge@0.3.0`, so the omp adapter
cannot arrive on a host until `0.2.1` publishes. The identity behaviour itself is
already proven locally on `fire` — blank cwd, corpus absent, three agents.
